\subsection{LSD-Studio: Producer UX and Mix Bus}
\label{subsec:arch_studio}

LSD-Studio~\cite{lsdstudio} is a Rust + \texttt{iced} desktop
application that wraps the Python LSD model workers and exposes the
full Spectral Composition workflow to the producer: library ingestion,
model baking, sound-bank auditioning, MIDI sequencing, recursive
generation, and multitrack mixing. The application builds to a single
self-contained binary; Python workers are embedded at compile time.

The desktop application is organised as a thin native frontend over
embedded model workers, communicating over an explicit IPC boundary:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=4mm and 10mm,
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners, minimum height=11mm, minimum width=33mm, align=center, font=\small},
  rpc/.style={draw=none, font=\scriptsize, align=center},
  arrow/.style={<->, >=stealth}
]
  \node[box] (ui) {LSD-Studio UI\\(\texttt{iced}, Rust)};
  \node[box, right=of ui] (core) {Studio core\\(bank, tracks, mix bus)};
  \node[box, below=9mm of ui] (rpc) {JSON-RPC\\bridge};
  \node[box, below=9mm of core] (pool) {Python worker pool\\(LSD \texttt{LSDModel})};
  \node[box, right=of pool] (torch) {PyTorch\\(MPS / CPU)};

  \draw[arrow] (ui) -- (core);
  \draw[arrow] (ui) -- (rpc);
  \draw[arrow] (rpc) -- (pool);
  \draw[arrow] (pool) -- (torch);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

The \texttt{iced} frontend owns all interaction state — library
ingestion, model baking progress, bank auditioning, MIDI sequencing,
track mixing — and never links PyTorch directly. Generative requests
(\texttt{generate\_sound\_bank}, \texttt{condition\_on\_audio},
\texttt{synthesize\_midi}) are serialised as JSON-RPC method calls to a
pool of Python workers, each holding one baked model
$\mathcal{M}$; responses stream back as chunked waveforms so the UI can
audition partial banks while later draws complete. The preset system
lives on the worker side: a preset is a frozen $(L_F, U_q,
\lambda^{\mathrm{ED}})$ plus trained decoder/DiT weights, selected at
run start; the mix bus lives entirely on the Rust side, so that a
worker crash costs generation but never the producer's mix state. This
split keeps the producer-facing surface rebuildable in isolation from
the research surface, and vice versa.


%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Implementation and Datasets}
\label{sec:implementation}
%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

